  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nRather progressive northern stream flow found across the northern\nConus/southern Canada early this afternoon, with embedded weak and\nmoisture starved wave racing east across the northern Great Lakes.\nJust enough forcing and Lake Superior moisture contribution to kick\noff a few light showers across parts of eastern upper this morning.\nOtherwise, main feature of interest is expansive are of high\npressure building into the western Great Lakes. Marginally gusty\nnorthwest winds on the front side of this high bringing a somewhat\ncooler airmass into the region, as evident by current temperatures\nmostly in the 50s (which are a few degrees below specific normal\nvalues for todays date).\n\nHigh pressure will continue to work east, centering itself directly\noverhead tonight...exiting stage right on Saturday. Upper level flow\nregime remains progressive, propelling next rather well organized\nshortwave trough into the area Saturday night. Detachment between\nnorthern stream energy and any deep tropical moisture connection\nonly increases with time, supporting mostly dry conditions as we\nhead through much of next week.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nInitial focus centers on temperature trends tonight and attendant\nfrost concerns. Secondary focus on weekend shower chances and next\nlikely more widespread frost/freeze producing temperatures Monday\nnight.\n\nDetails:\n\nAny shower threat comes to an end this afternoon across eastern\nupper Michigan with departure of shortwave trough. As mentioned,\nupstream high pressure centers itself directly overhead tonight,\nbringing clearing skies and light/calm wind as it does so. This\nshould set the stage for a good to excellent nocturnal temperature\nresponse. However, still have some concern that wet soils from\nrecent rains (especially south of the big bridge) will prevent an\nall out temperature free-fall. Little doubt however that frost\nproducing temperatures in the lower to middle 30s will be realized\nacross eastern upper Michigan and away from the Great Lake collar\ncounties of northern lower. Very possible some of our traditional \ncolder locations along M-28 in eastern upper (looking at you Raco)\nand within the Au Sable River valley of northern lower dip down into\nthe upper 20s. Appropriate frost headlines already issued by the\novernight crew (many thanks to them).\n\nNice temperature recovery on Saturday as winds turn south ahead of\nthe next wave. This wave will bring our next chance for showers\nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Juxtaposition of best forcing\nand limited moisture centers itself north of the bridge Saturday\nnight, suggesting that is where best shower chances will\nreside...with shower chances decreasing the further south one\ntravels. Another large area of high pressure follows this wave\nSunday night, moving across the Northwoods to kick off next work\nweek. Simple magnitude of cold air advection supports lows back\ndown into the 30s Sunday night. Pattern recognition strongly supports\neven colder conditions Monday night as that chilly high centers\noverhead. Early progs support widespread sub-freezing lows across\ninterior areas by early Tuesday morning. Rest of the week looks\nrather uneventful, although maturing return flow on backside of\nretreating high will likely bring a return to above normal\ntemperatures on Wednesday. Fast moving and moisture starved waves\nmay bring some additional late week shower chances, but definitely\nnot seeing any evidence of widespread rains.",
